    "filename": "05-futures-rollover-analysis-smart-money-carry.md",
    "markdown": "# Futures Rollover Analysis: How to Read Monthly Expiry Carry-Forward Positions of Big Institutions\n\n*Target Keywords: Futures rollover analysis NSE, what is rollover in stock market, rollover percentage calculation, how to read rollover data, roll spread cost of carry, monthly expiry trading strategy*  \n*Published on TradersDesk Research &bull; 9 min read &bull; Level: Intermediate to Advanced*\n\n---\n\n> **TL;DR (Quick Summary for Busy Traders):**  \n> Every monthly futures contract expires on the last Thursday of the month. Big institutions like FIIs and DIIs cannot sell thousands of crores overnight, so they \"roll over\" their trades into the next month by closing current-month contracts and reopening next-month contracts. If a stock has a **High Rollover %** and traders are willing to pay a **Higher Premium (Positive Spread)**, it means big money is aggressively bullish for the upcoming month. TradersDesk Rollover Desk compares current rollovers against 3-month benchmarks to give you an early edge.\n\n---\n\n## What is Futures Rollover in Plain English?\n\nIn the Indian stock market, equity and index futures contracts have an expiration date: the **last Thursday of every month**.\n\nWhen expiry week arrives, hedge funds, mutual funds, and large trading institutions face a choice:\n\n1. **Square Off (Close Trade)**: Take their profit or loss and leave the market completely.\n2. **Roll Over (Carry Forward)**: Close the contract expiring this Thursday and immediately open the exact same position in next month’s contract.\n\n```\n+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                         THE ROLLOVER TRANSACTION                                |\n+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Near-Month Contract (Expiring This Thursday)  -->  SELL TO CLOSE (-1 Lot)       |\n| Next-Month Contract (Expiring Next Month)     -->  BUY TO OPEN (+1 Lot)        |\n+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| RESULT: The position is successfully \"Rolled Over\" into the new series!       |\n+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n```\n\nBecause institutions handle thousands of crores of capital, **how they roll over their contracts reveals their directional bias for the next 30 to 60 days**.\n\n---\n\n## 1. The Two Core Metrics: Rollover % and Roll Spread\n\nTo read rollover data accurately, you only need to understand two numbers:\n\n### A. Rollover Percentage (Roll %)\nRollover percentage measures how many of the expiring contracts have been carried forward into the next month:\n\n$$\\text{Rollover \\%} = \\frac{\\text{Next Month OI} + \\text{Far Month OI}}{\\text{Total Combined OI Across All Months}} \\times 100$$\n\n* **High Rollover (Above 3-Month Average)**: Big institutions have high conviction in the existing trend and are keeping their positions alive.\n* **Low Rollover (Below 3-Month Average)**: Traders are closing positions and sitting in cash; signals trend exhaustion or uncertainty.\n\n### B. Rollover Cost of Carry (Roll Spread / Basis)\nCarrying a position over is not free. The **Roll Spread** is the price difference between the next-month contract and the current-month contract:\n\n$$\\text{Roll Spread} = \\text{Next Month Futures Price} - \\text{Near Month Futures Price}$$\n\n* **Positive Roll Spread (Premium)**: Next month trades at a higher price (e.g., +₹8.00). Buyers are willing to pay extra to hold their long bets.\n* **Negative Roll Spread (Discount)**: Next month trades at a discount (e.g., -₹5.00). Short sellers are aggressively paying to maintain their bearish bets.\n\n---\n\n## 2. The Institutional Rollover Matrix\n\nCombining **Rollover %** with **Roll Spread** gives you four clear market signals:\n\n```\n+-------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                  THE INSTITUTIONAL ROLLOVER MATRIX                      |\n+-------------------+---------------------+-------------------------------+\n| ROLLOVER %        | ROLL SPREAD (BASIS) | INSTITUTIONAL INTERPRETATION  |\n+-------------------+---------------------+-------------------------------+\n| HIGH (> 3M Avg)   | WIDE PREMIUM (▲)    | 🚀 AGGRESSIVE LONG ROLLOVER   |\n| HIGH (> 3M Avg)   | DISCOUNT (▼)        | 🔻 AGGRESSIVE SHORT ROLLOVER  |\n| LOW (< 3M Avg)    | WIDE PREMIUM (▲)    | ⚠️ PROFIT BOOKING ON LONGS     |\n| LOW (< 3M Avg)    | DISCOUNT (▼)        | 🔄 SHORT COVERING / EXIT      |\n+-------------------+---------------------+-------------------------------+\n```\n\n### 1. High Rollover + Premium = Aggressive Long Roll\nWhen both the rollover percentage and the roll spread are higher than average, institutional buyers are eager to ride the uptrend into the new month. High probability of continued upside.\n\n### 2. High Rollover + Discount = Aggressive Short Roll\nWhen rollover is high but next month's contract is trading at a discount, big funds are rolling over their short positions. They expect deeper price drops in the coming weeks.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Real Example: The Tata Motors Expiry Surge\n\nHere is a real market scenario from recent series:\n\n1. **The Context**: Tata Motors had gained +12% during the month. Heading into expiry week, retail traders thought the stock was \"overbought\" and began taking short positions.\n2. **What TradersDesk Rollover Desk Showed**:\n   - **Historical 3-Month Average Roll**: 74%\n   - **Current Expiry Roll by Wednesday Afternoon**: **86.4%** (Massive Long Roll!)\n   - **Roll Spread**: Expanded from +₹2.50 to +₹6.80 premium!\n3. **The Institutional Message**: Big funds were not booking profits. They paid top dollar to carry their long contracts forward.\n4. **The Outcome**: In the first 10 sessions of the new series, the stock surged another **+9.5%**, crushing retail short-sellers.\n\n---\n\n## Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs)\n\n### What does \"Rollover\" mean in stock futures?\nRollover is the process of closing an expiring derivatives contract in the current month and simultaneously opening an identical position in the next month's contract to maintain the trade.\n\n### When does futures rollover take place in India?\nFutures rollover happens during the final week of every monthly series, reaching its peak activity between Wednesday afternoon and Thursday 03:30 PM (the monthly expiry day).\n\n### What is a good rollover percentage?\nA \"good\" rollover percentage depends on the individual stock's history. Generally, an index rollover above 75%-80% or a stock rollover higher than its 3-month historical benchmark indicates strong institutional participation.\n\n### Where can I check live rollover percentages and roll spreads for NSE stocks?\nYou can track real-time rollover progress, comparative 3-month averages, and roll spreads for all 200+ F&O stocks on the **[TradersDesk Rollover Desk](https://tradersdesk.in/rollover)**.\n"
